From u3fe90052fbf2f899 at groups.google.com  Fri Mar  4 14:52:37 2016
From: u3fe90052fbf2f899 at groups.google.com (Terrence Jorgensen)
Date: Fri, 04 Mar 2016 14:52:37 +0000
Subject: Re: comparing models with binomial variables - AIC, or alternatives?
Message-ID: <3iR20nSl6fM.9RM5MKu1AwAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <3iR20nSl6fM.zsOrudINAwAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
References: <3iR20nSl6fM.zsOrudINAwAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/3iR20nSl6fM/m/9RM5MKu1AwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

> A final question: to make the correct comparison for my case (i.e. whether the grouping factor increases the fit of the model), should I constrain "loadings", "intercepts", "regression", or all? (although the model with the last option did not converge).

An omnibus test of equality of covariance matrices across groups would require you to constrain all parameters.   To make sure it is working correctly, I suggest counting the number of parameters in your single-group model, and making sure that's how many more degrees of freedom the constrained 2-group model has than the unconstrained 2-group model.

But you can also test group differences of specific parameters -- perhaps as a follow-up test to a significance chi-squared so you can see which parameters seem to differ and which constraints seem tenable.  FYI, you don't have any "loadings" in your model because there are only manifest variables.  I think you want to use group.equal = c("regressions","residuals","intercepts"), but that might not constrain the (co)variances of your exogenous variables (fat + muscle + wing_length), assuming you want fixed.x = FALSE.  But if you want to treat exogenous means/(co)variances as known instead of random, then that's not a problem.

Terry

From u3fe90052fbf2f899 at groups.google.com  Fri Mar  4 15:05:31 2016
From: u3fe90052fbf2f899 at groups.google.com (Terrence Jorgensen)
Date: Fri, 04 Mar 2016 15:05:31 +0000
Subject: Re: Moderation variable in SEM
Message-ID: <OA14tHqjd50.P958O1-2AwAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <OA14tHqjd50.GFAgleSjAwAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
References: <OA14tHqjd50.GFAgleSjAwAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/OA14tHqjd50/m/P958O1-2AwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

> Now I would like to add a moderator variable to this direct effect - demotion - which is also a latent construct.

You cannot use the asterisk in lavaan syntax to automatically calculate product terms as you would in a formula object (e.g., for lm() or glm() models).  When you have an interaction among observed predictors, you would need to calculate the product term and store it as a variable in your data set, then include that product term in your regression formula in the lavaan syntax.

But you cannot use lavaan syntax to estimate an interaction involving a latent predictor.  You can look into the nlsem package to use the latent moderated structures (LMS) approach, which Mplus implements.  Or you can look into the semTools package for helpful functions to implement the product-indicator approach in lavaan.  (Look up the ?indProd help page for details and references describing the method.)

Terry

